<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("locality_emergence") %>
<% radii = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("radius") %>
<% seeds = sweep.fetch(:seeds) %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% rate_exponent = -Math.log2(Lab::EMERGENT_MUTATION_RATE).round %>
<% record_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main/docs/design_record.md" %>
<% constants = Lab::LocalityEmergenceReading %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  Rung 0 of the programme asks under what conditions a replicator arises at all. The
  <%= link_to "radius sweep", finding_path("radius-locality") %> asked whether a cell's reach
  decides it and could not tell: ten seeds an arm, one or two transitions in each. The
  <%= link_to "lineage-diversity sweep", finding_path("lineages-after-emergence") %>, built to
  ask something else, then saw radius 4 emerge four times as often as radius 1 and five times
  as often as the well-mixed world. DESIGN §1.3 sweep 13 puts that pattern to fresh worlds:
  <strong>does emergence peak at an intermediate reach?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Sweep 12's world to the byte — a 128×128 torus of tapes held at 64 bytes, at the emergent
  mutation rate <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span>, with
  <span class="mono">lineage_rule = oriented</span> — at radius
  <span class="mono"><%= radii.reject(&:zero?).to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  and 0, the well-mixed world. <%= seeds.size %> seeds per arm, <%= epochs %> epochs per run,
  <%= number_with_delimiter radii.size * seeds.size %> runs. The seeds are
  <%= seeds.first %>–<%= seeds.last %>, not sweep 12's 1–90: the lineage rule changes no byte
  of a world, so those seeds at radius 1, 2, 4 and 0 would have replayed sweep 12's worlds and
  its exploratory counts with them.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong>, with every number fixed before a run was
  read: <%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-09-27, "Does emergence peak at
  an intermediate reach?". A run is emerged where it has a confirmed emergence epoch and reads
  <span class="mono">replicator_share</span> of at least
  <%= percent_value constants::MIN_SHARE %> at some sample from it on — sweep 12's rule,
  unchanged, on the orientation-aware share. <strong>H-peak</strong>, the confirmatory test:
  radius <%= constants::PEAK_RADIUS %> emerges more often than radius 1 and than the
  well-mixed world, two one-sided Fisher exact tests Holm-corrected at family level
  <%= constants::FAMILY_ALPHA %>. <strong>H-shape</strong>: over the finite radii, a logistic
  regression of emergence on radius and radius² bends down, its radius² coefficient negative
  at a two-sided Wald p below <%= constants::SHAPE_LEVEL %>, with its fitted peak strictly
  between radius <%= constants::SHAPE_PEAK_FLOOR %> and <%= constants::SHAPE_PEAK_CEILING %>
  so that two of the radii read lie on each side of it. A curve that only rises or only falls
  across the radii is not a peak.
</p>

<% reading = @show.locality_emergence %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.any? %>
  <p>
    <span class="badge <%= reading.peak.badge_class %>">H-peak <%= reading.peak.outcome_label %></span>
    <span class="badge <%= reading.shape.badge_class %>">H-shape <%= reading.shape.outcome_label %></span>
    <strong><%= reading.claim_sentence %></strong>
  </p>
  <% if reading.interim? %>
    <p>Not every run of the sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.</p>
  <% end %>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored runs at render time, through the
    same reading <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("locality-emergence", anchor: "locality-emergence-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Emergence by reach">
    <table class="data-table" id="locality-emergence-curve">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Arm</th>
          <th class="numeric">Finished</th>
          <th class="numeric">Confirmed crossing</th>
          <th class="numeric">Emerged</th>
          <th class="numeric">Rate</th>
          <% if reading.exploratory? %>
            <th class="numeric">Sweep 12 crossing</th>
            <th class="numeric">Sweep 12 emerged</th>
          <% end %>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <% reading.arms.each do |arm| %>
          <tr>
            <td><%= arm.label %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.finished %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.crossed %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= arm.emerged %> of <%= arm.finished %></td>
            <td class="numeric"><%= percent_value arm.rate&.to_f %></td>
            <% if reading.exploratory? %>
              <td class="numeric"><%= arm.exploratory? ? arm.exploratory.crossed : "—" %></td>
              <td class="numeric"><%= arm.exploratory? ? "#{arm.exploratory.emerged} of #{arm.exploratory.finished}" : "—" %></td>
            <% end %>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    A confirmed crossing that never read a share of one half after it is not emerged: the
    census that confirms a crossing is blind to reverse copiers, and the share clause asks
    that the world was, at some point, mostly replicators. Well-mixed is not a distance, so it
    stands at the end of the table and outside the fit.
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/locality_emergence_reading", reading: reading.report %>

  <% if reading.same_total_arms.any? %>
    <h2 class="mt-6">The same totals, not the same worlds</h2>
    <p id="locality-emergence-coincidence">
      At <%= reading.same_total_arms.map(&:label).to_sentence %> this sweep emerged exactly as
      often as sweep 12 did:
      <%= reading.same_total_arms.map { |arm| "#{arm.emerged} of #{arm.finished}" }.to_sentence %>.
      That is a coincidence of totals, not reused data. This sweep's worlds are seeds
      <%= reading.seed_range.first %>–<%= reading.seed_range.last %> and sweep 12's are
      <%= reading.exploratory_seed_range.first %>–<%= reading.exploratory_seed_range.last %>,
      so no world is shared, and every run is its own row under its own experiment. The
      crossings say the same: <%= reading.same_total_arms.map { |arm| "#{arm.crossed} against #{arm.exploratory.crossed} at #{arm.label}" }.to_sentence %>,
      and where a count of crossings agrees the seeds and epochs that crossed do not. H-peak and
      H-shape read this sweep's runs alone; sweep 12's only suggested the question.
    </p>
  <% end %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("locality-emergence") %> is in this
    database, so there is no arm to read here. This page reads the arms at render time and
    states the claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>How it reads against the earlier findings</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Does spatial locality buy emergence?", finding_path("radius-locality") %> read
  locality as not speeding emergence, on ten seeds an arm at the engine's default rate and on
  the detector's crossing alone. It could rule out an enormous effect and nothing smaller, and
  it saw no peak because one or two events an arm cannot draw one. This sweep reads nine times
  the seeds at the emergent rate and finds the effect that one could not see — not a monotone
  benefit of locality, which the tightest reach does not have, but a best reach in the middle.
  <%= link_to "After emergence, one lineage takes the world at every reach", finding_path("lineages-after-emergence") %>
  is the other half: reach changes how often a replicator arises, not what follows it, for once
  one has arisen a single lineage holds the world whatever the reach.
</p>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>One world.</strong> Every run is 128×128, at one mutation rate, with tapes held at
    64 bytes and a budget of <%= epochs %> epochs. Where the peak sits in another world size, at
    another rate or with tapes that can grow is untested, and a reach of 4 means something
    different on a larger torus.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Emergence is the replicator-world definition.</strong> A world counts where the
    detector confirmed a crossing and replicators then held half of it. A crossing alone is not
    what is claimed: every arm crossed more often than it emerged.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No mechanism.</strong> Nothing here says why a reach near 4 is best: whether a
    tight neighbourhood starves a copier of partners, a wide one dilutes it before it takes
    hold, or something else. The sweep measured the curve, not its cause.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Well-mixed is not on the radius scale.</strong> It is radius 0 in the parameters
    and a partner drawn from the whole torus in the world. It is compared with radius
    <%= constants::PEAK_RADIUS %> in H-peak and left out of the fit, and it is not the point
    past radius 8.
  </li>
</ul>
